 factors which  constitute cells micro environment  as seen in the schematic cell  experiences blood flow  shear stress pressure drop stretching  interaction with physical barriers and  collectively imparting physical forces  on the cells  further there is concentration gradients  exchange of gases  there are chemical signaling molecules  and exothermic  cellular molecules which constitutes the  biochemical factors  and lastly physiochemical factors such  as temperature ph  collectively interact and affect the  cells and yet in such a dynamic system  cells are able to replicate and perform  its designated function  uh but in traditional dish space or  you know now conventional assays these  functions such as sensing replication  exchange of materials might be altered  as cells do not experience  a dynamic environment it is known that  cell  exchange morphologies when cells  i mean i'm sorry it is known that cell  changes morphologies when exposed to  substrate deformation  but cells in culture play do not  experience these forces and eventually  lose these properties  therefore studying dynamic system for  studying cellular microenvironment  without compromising natural properties  and behavior of the cells is crucial  so how can we study cell dynamic systems  and one of the one of the answers is  biofluidics  so what is microfluidics microfluidics  is study and manipulation of small  volumes of fluid  in channels and chamber with dimensions  of tens of microliters  microfluidic cell culture attempts to  develop devices and techniques for  culturing  maintaining analyzing and experimenting  cells in microscale volume  now using this microfluidic techniques  we can actually  we can actually make devices uh to carry  out 2d and 3d cell culture systems  in the following image shows a  microfluidic chip comprising of inlets  for media  a culture chamber in which cells can be  grown and  outlet for removal of spent media so  such chips can be used  to culture cancer cells for assessing  various different cellular activities  so now let us see what are the  differences between in vitro culture  and microfluidic cell culture system in  in vitro system the cell substrate is  transparent stiff and has low gas  permeability  while microbic systems the substrate is  soft  and is flexible and also has high gas  permeability  in mathematics system cells can be  handled and cultured  ranging from single to thousand cell  which is actually not possible in a  plate based assay  conventional culture is carried out in  flask dish and plates which require  larger volume as compared to  microfluidic devices  which can work with volumes as less as  60 nanoliters  also nutrients in traditional culture  are in great access and media needs  change after every 48  hours while in mathematics system media  turnover is faster and some reports  indicate more glucose consumption  one particular thing about microfluidic  cell culture is the proliferation rate  and in conventional assays the  proliferation  the rate is typically between 18 to 24  hours  but in microfluidic system there have  been reports where  it has been shown less as well as faster  proliferation rates of the cells  so how do we culture cells in  microfluidic systems  culturing cells and microbiotic devices  require a strong understanding of  certain fundamental principles spanning  disciplines such as physics chemistry  biology and engineering  so it's more of an interdisciplinary  kind of study  and there are certain criterias which  need to be followed firstly the  developed system should closely mimic  conditions  that exist in vivo and the person  handling should have  good command on cell culture techniques  to translate methods from macro to a  microscope  i mean to micro to macro scale and this  is usually the most challenging part  also another criteria is person should  have  a strong background in designing and  microfabrication techniques  so let us see what are the steps  involved in establishing a successful  microfluidic culture  so the first step obviously is the  fabrication of the device this is  typically carried out  by techniques such as photolithography  and soft lithography  after which you have to select the type  of cell which you are going to  investigate  so it can be cancelled non-cancerous  cells  and based on their properties such as  earlier and or non-earlier end  we might need to fabricate a different  or a new disk in a microfluidic check  then we need to perfuse media into the  chip which is usually  done using syringe pump or paraslatic  pumps  the chips are then monitored using  microscopic technique  we usually use light cell microscopy in  our lab  and at last the span meter is collected  which contains all the extracellular  components and dead cells  so let us see the first step which is  the fabrication process in  detail so microfabrication process is  divided into two parts one is  photolithography and another is soft  lithography so  as the name suggests photolithography  is a technique which uses uh which is  used to edge surface using light and  this is usually  a uv source basically in little in  photolithography we have a mask which is  nothing but a stencil on which the  design of the microphotic chip is edged  on a silicon software  substrate which is called the wafer a  layer of photoresist is coated  we in our lab we use su-8 for this after  which the mask is aligned on the  substrate and uv  is exposed once exposed for a definite  time the uv light passes to the stem  cell or the mask  and erodes the photoresist at the point  creating design  on the mask onto the substrate after  etching the final baking and development  is carried out  to obtain the substrate with the design  further pdms which is a  known polymer it's called polymethyl  philoxane  is added to the substrate and allowed to  cure once pdma solidifies  it is it is peeled off and the  replicated design  is uh and the design gets replicated on  pdms  in case of soft lithography the move is  first prepared  and then pdms is just added into the  mold and cured  the ems is a room temperature  vulcanizing rubber that solidifies the  mixed with curing agent  and this process can be accelerated by  applying heat so after turing  the pdms is beyond filled and we are  left with design pattern  on the pdms  further this is the process of  photolithography so as i explained  earlier  in the first step the silicon wafer is  removed from the clean room  it is kept on the spin coater in the  third step the photoresist such as suv  polymer is added  and it spin at desired rpm to achieve a  specific thickness  then it is pre-baked after this step the  mask is selected  step 6 and 7 is aligning of the mask  using a mask aligner  in step 8 uv is exposed and finally  in step 10 the mask the exposed surface  is washed  finally we have the desired geometry on  the wafer  now this acts as a mold and pdm is added  on the wafer  which is allowed to cure and the  microfluidic chip is peeled  so in the last image you can see the  design of the microfluidic chips and the  channel  in case of soft lithography the process  is quite simple and i would like to  share one step protocol which we use in  a lab  design circular micro micro channels and  chambers  the protocol goes when the protocol  describes  using agar rose powder which is readily  available which you can mix with water  and allow it to boil  we then cut the tape of syringe  desperate this agarose powder and allow  it to cool  once full with the black jar push the  agarose out from the syringe to  cylindrical agarose shape cut the  agarose into desired height and pass  copper wire through the upper cylinder  then we place this entire setup into a  box and you just add pdms into it  you keep the entire setup at 70 degrees  for 3 hours which is the curing process  and later once pdms is solidified you  just remove  the chip and boil it in water the  agarose will melt off  and you can simply pull off the wire  having circular channels and chambers  and this can be used to culture culture  cells i mean  so there are certain parameters which  needs to optimize for successful cell  culture  and these parameters are first is the  flow rate  so fluorite can can be set using syringe  pump or parasitic pumps  usually fluorides can be said to mimic  actual flow condition in blood vessels  and capillaries which range from 1 to  2 000 microliters per minute second is  the temperature and co2 control  cells require physiological temperature  and co2 control therefore  you can use a pid controller with  heating plate coupled with co2  controller  or there is also an automated onstage  incubator with co2 systems  which are available which can be mounted  on microscope to perform light cell  imaging  and lastly we also need  to monitor the cells for which you can  use inverted microscope or even  microscope so using these techniques  we attempted to culture cells in a 2d  platform  in the first image you can see the  microscopic chip  which was fabricated using  photolithography the chip consists of  inlets and outlets  which are interconnected to small  chambers which are 100 micron  in height and 3 mm in diameter  media containing dmu and f12  supplemented with 10  fbs was used for the experiments and we  selected mdmb  231 cells for our study after the chip  was fabricated  the chip was coated with polyalycin and  fiber netting to allow  cells to adhere then mdmb231 cells were  inoculated in the channel and allowed to  integrate  intubate for two to four hours this is  crucial  since cells need some time to substrate  and we cannot immediately start the flow  rate  after two to four hours challenge with  the media  is placed in the pump and at a set added  at a desired flow rate and connected to  the chip  uh we need to take care not to  uh not to set the flow rate too high  because  that would end up leading formation of  bubbles  which is a major challenge in  microfluidic 2d and 3d cell culture  systems  further we incubate the system at 37  degrees celsius with 5  co2 we monitored the cells by recording  time-lapse imaging of the cells after  every 3 minutes for 12 hours  at different flow rates and i have a  sample video  which shows the migration of the cells  the flow rate was 3 microliters per  minute and from our data we came to a  conclusion that fluorite has negligible  effects on cell migration  uh in this time lapse it is seen that  cells are growing in flow condition but  are not affected by flow rate  rather we can also see some cells which  are migrating in the opposite direction  of the flow  uh also it was noted that cell  proliferation was much slower  although these findings are still under  investigation but our initial proof of  concept  further we also found that cells do tend  to adhere to the side walls of the  microfluidic chip as compared to the  central region  this is probably due to low pressure at  the side walls as compared to the  central region  and also due to high surface roughness  at the edges which provide better  contact points for the cell  another recent investigation which we  are working on is testing of different  drug-loaded nanoparticles on cancer  cells  in our finding post-6r treatment  cellular death and cell shrinking was  observed  but strikingly after 12 hours uh  we do observe some cells trying to  re-establish and polypheny  which might be useful to explore drug  resistance and  uneven diffusion of drug and continuous  flow  this might help develop better drug  molecules to treat cancer  although these studies look simpler  there are several challenges which are  encountered in microfluidic system  the major being controlling evaporation  rate and generation of bubbles  so in the image c you can see that you  usually end up having these bubbles in  the microfluidics  chips which is a major challenge but  overall if these problems are taken 